A||||5||AkursedX||8 inch rims in rear?||||5||
Z||000000||AkursedX||01-12-2001||11:09 PM||||Hello all,<P>Soon I am going to be ordering my new 17 inch rims or my 88 GT.  But I have a question.<P>The type of rims that I want (Konig Tantrums) come in 7 and 8 inch widths.  Now my idea was to get the 7 inch for the front and get 8 inch rims for the rear.  Would there be any adverse affects to doing this in respect to hitting wheelwells, or any other parts?  I don't know if it would matter, but my car is lowered 1 inch as well.<P>My second question is, what size tires for 8 inch rims?  225's or 235's?  <P>Any info is appreciated, thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||bubbajoexx||01-12-2001||11:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AkursedX:<BR><B>Hello all,<P><BR>Soon I am going to be ordering my new 17 inch rims or my 88 GT.  But I have a question.<P><BR>The type of rims that I want (Konig Tantrums) come in 7 and 8 inch widths.  Now my idea was to get the 7 inch for the front and get 8 inch rims for the rear.  Would there be any adverse affects to doing this in respect to hitting wheelwells, or any other parts?  I don't know if it would matter, but my car is lowered 1 inch as well.<P><BR>My second question is, what size tires for 8 inch rims?  225's or 235's?  <P><BR>Any info is appreciated, thanks   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Have your rim supplier set you up with the right backspace and you will have no problem with 8" rims I use 275/35/17 on my car||||reg||1
Z||000002||stomper282||01-13-2001||01:02 AM||||hey I work for Americas Tire Co. I have delt with the diffrent fitments on fieros&lt; right now I am running 18x8 all around and I'm widening(sp) my rears to 9's so I can fit 275/35-18 in the rear. Back to the subject... 17x8 Konig Tantrums will fit all around, the benifit of that would be the ability to rotoate you tires. Which would save you money in the long run on tires!! <BR> If you live around an Americas/Discount Tire Co. they will save you money as well. <BR>Butthats my very bias opinion  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000003||Will||01-13-2001||11:34 AM||||Are you sure that 275/35-18's will fit?<P>If they'll fit, there shouldn't be any reason 275/40-17's will fit, right?<BR>I want to use that size because it's a Corvette size and pretty common.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric||01-13-2001||11:53 AM||||AkursedX, tell me how you lowered your 88.||||reg||1
Z||000005||AkursedX||01-13-2001||04:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Eric:<BR><B>AkursedX, tell me how you lowered your 88.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well my whole list of suspension work is Polyurethane all around.  KYB's in the front and rear, the Fierostore's sport spring kit, and new polyurethane swaybar endlinks.  <P>I haven't done the rear yet, but i did the front and it has dropped about an inch.  If I had to do it again, I would look for springs with maybe a 1.5 inch drop.  I think it would still look better with a little less fender gap.<P><BR>But anyways, it sounds like I'll have no problems with the 8 inch rims in the rear. I would put 8 inch ones in the front, but I prefer the look of a bigger rim in the rear and smaller in the front.  Plus I will still hopefully retain some understeer (I'm used to it and I prefer driving that way) I can live without tire rotations, I have gotten used to it already because my '88 has different sized rims to begin with.<P>Thanks for the input!<BR>||||reg||1
